					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aelanna" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(334454); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=334454&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605948049" alt="Aelanna" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=334454)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aelanna" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(334454); return false;" >Aelanna</a></span> <span class="odate time_1392308322 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Feb 2014 16:18</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1966105">
									

<p>A couple of minor sticking points:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Haliaeetus leucocephalus</em> should probably be qualified with its common name (bald eagle). Peer-reviewed scientific documents do this in real life, because even Real Scientists™ can't be arsed to Google these things.</li>
<li>Bald eagles are not small birds. How does one accidentally ingest a bald eagle? (Admittedly, I may not want to know the answer to this.)</li>
<li>MTF should probably be spelled out as Mobile Task Force the first time it's used in a document, just for clinicality's sake.</li>
<li>MTF Chi-3 is one of the example task forces in <a href="/task-forces">Task Forces</a>. While you're certainly not obligated to change this, I did spend a lot of time making sure that nothing on that guide clashed with existing usage. :)</li>
<li>A significant amount of time was spent trying to ensure that the various nicknames for the example task forces were suitably conversational, meaning that it's reasonably non-silly to say things like, &quot;we need to call in the Exorcists&quot; or &quot;it's already being handled by the Debuggers&quot;. &quot;Mops and Buckets&quot; doesn't lend itself to casual usage very well.</li>
<li>Very minor personal point as well, it seems really strange for two separate Mobile Task Forces to be mobilized for a single SCP; what's the point of a specialized task force if it can only monitor or handle a situation, but not both? Another option is that Foundation field agents across the world monitor for emergence, but a task force is responsible for handling it.</li>
<li>&quot;auditory memetic cognitohazard&quot; is a bit weird, since &quot;memetic agent&quot; already implies &quot;cognitohazard&quot;. By common usage, &quot;auditory memetic agent&quot; works just fine.</li>
<li>All memetic agents are self-propagating. Did you mean &quot;spontaneous outbreak&quot; instead?</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
